<description>
Mark the disagreement between two same-size foreground masks separated by a center bar.

Input structure
The grid has odd width. The middle column is a single-color separator, with
equal-size binary masks on its left and right. The two masks share one
background color and each side has exactly one foreground color.

Rule
Remove the separator and compare the two side masks cell by cell. Output the
shared background color except where exactly one side has foreground; those
differing cells become red.

Key insight
The separator is not part of the answer; it aligns two colored masks whose
exclusive-or is rendered as a compact red mask.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "two equal-size binary foreground masks separated by a full-height one-pixel bar",
  "transform": "drop the separator; for each aligned mask pixel, output red if exactly one side has foreground and otherwise output background",
  "grid_shape": "same height as input and width equal to one side mask"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 5 rows x 9 cols (odd width) [observed]
  layout: left_panel | separator_col | right_panel, each panel 5x4 [observed]
  separator:
    single solid-color column at center (col W//2)
    color: YELLOW(4) [observed]; must differ from bg and not appear in either panel
  panels:
    each is a binary mask — exactly 1 background color + 1 foreground color
    bg: BLACK(0) [observed]; shared by both panels (exactly 1 common color)
    left_fg: AZURE(8), right_fg: GREY(5) [observed]; must differ from each other and from bg/sep
    foreground density: 40-60% per panel [observed]
  overlap: panels share 4-7 foreground cells at same positions [observed]
  output:
    same size as one panel (5x4)
    XOR of the two binary masks — cells where exactly one panel has fg become RED(2), rest become bg
    red cell count: 9-13 [observed]

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (constraints are simple enough for forward generation)
  steps:
    1. Pick grid height H in 4-7, panel width W in 3-6, so input is H x (2W+1).
    2. Choose 4 distinct colors — bg, left_fg, right_fg, separator — with bg=BLACK, others from non-BLACK palette [observed].
    3. Generate left binary mask — fill H x W panel with bg, randomly set 40-60% of cells to left_fg.
    4. Generate right binary mask — fill H x W panel with bg, randomly set 40-60% of cells to right_fg.
    5. Ensure at least 1 cell where both are fg (overlap) and at least 1 cell where exactly one is fg (non-trivial XOR output).
    6. Concatenate left_panel, separator column, right_panel to form input grid.
    7. Output = XOR mask — bg where both agree, RED where exactly one has fg.
